/* User guide */
"help.toc" = "Contents";
"help.quickStart.title" = "Quick start";
"help.quickStart.step1.title" = "Grant Full Disk Access";
"help.quickStart.step1.body" = "Open System Settings › Privacy & Security › Full Disk Access and turn on Clean My Mac. Without it some folders are skipped and results are incomplete.";
"help.quickStart.step2.title" = "Press “Scan All” (⌘R)";
"help.quickStart.step2.body" = "Internal drives are scanned right away. If external drives are connected, the app asks which ones to include. You can cancel at any time (⌘.).";
"help.quickStart.step3.title" = "Read the Summary";
"help.quickStart.step3.body" = "Findings are sorted by severity: Critical, Warning, Tip. Press “Show” to jump to the folder, Docker or duplicate list behind each one.";
"help.quickStart.step4.title" = "Clean up safely";
"help.quickStart.step4.body" = "Everything you remove goes to the Trash and can be restored with “Put Back” in Finder. System folders are locked and cannot be removed by mistake.";
"help.fullDiskAccess.title" = "Full Disk Access";
"help.fullDiskAccess.body" = "macOS protects some folders (Mail, Messages, Safari, other apps’ containers). Apps can read them only with Full Disk Access.\n\n1. Open System Settings › Privacy & Security › Full Disk Access.\n2. Turn on Clean My Mac (use “+” to add it if it is not listed).\n3. Return to the app and press “Check Again”. If it still shows “Not granted”, quit and reopen the app.\n\nClean My Mac only reads file names and sizes. Nothing is uploaded anywhere.";
"help.scanning.title" = "Scanning drives";
"help.scanning.body" = "“Scan All” scans the startup disk and other internal drives. External drives (USB, SSD, SD card) are listed first and only scanned after you confirm — you can tick which ones. Choose “Remember my choice” to skip the question next time (change it later in Settings).\n\nWhen you plug in a drive while the app is open, it offers to scan it.\n\nSizes are the space actually used on disk. Hard-linked files are counted once, symbolic links are not followed and other drives are never entered, so totals are not inflated.\n\nThe “System data & unreadable” part of the bar is space macOS uses that cannot be listed: APFS snapshots, swap, protected system folders.";
"help.severity.title" = "Severity levels";
"help.severity.body" = "Each finding has a level so you can see what matters first.\n\nSSDs slow down when they are nearly full: macOS needs free space on the startup disk for virtual memory (swap), caches, APFS snapshots and system updates. When space runs out, apps freeze and updates fail.\n\nThe limits below can be changed in Settings.";
"help.severity.tableTitle" = "Recommended free space";
"help.severity.critical" = "Startup disk below %@, or any drive below %@ free";
"help.severity.warning" = "Startup disk below %@, or any drive below %@ free";
"help.severity.ok" = "Above those limits. Large cleanable folders are shown as Tips.";
"help.folders.title" = "Folders & treemap";
"help.folders.body" = "Open a drive and choose “Folders”. The list is sorted largest first; the treemap shows the same folders as rectangles sized by space.\n\nDouble-click a folder (in the list or the treemap) to open it, use the path at the top to go back. Right-click for Show in Finder, Quick Look and Move to Trash.\n\n“Categories” shows space by purpose (apps, photos, developer files, caches…). “Large Files” lists the biggest files on the drive.";
"help.duplicates.title" = "Duplicate file names";
"help.duplicates.body" = "After scanning, files that share the same name on any scanned drive are grouped together. Names are compared ignoring upper/lower case and Vietnamese accent encoding.\n\nGroups are sorted by how much space you would free by keeping only the newest copy. By default node_modules, .git, build folders, app bundles, ~/Library and system folders are skipped, and files under 1 MB are ignored.\n\nImportant: the same name does not mean the same content. Use Quick Look on each copy before moving anything to the Trash.";
"help.docker.title" = "Cleaning Docker";
"help.docker.body" = "The Docker page works step by step:\n1. Is Docker installed? If not, nothing else happens and no command is run.\n2. Is Docker running? If not, open Docker Desktop (or OrbStack/Colima) and press “Check Again”.\n3. List images tagged <none> (dangling) — leftovers from rebuilding images that no container uses.\n4. Remove the ones you select, after confirmation.\n\nImages are removed without --force, so Docker refuses to remove any image still used by a container.\n\nWith Docker Desktop, the virtual disk file Docker.raw may not shrink right away after cleaning.";
"help.deleting.title" = "Deleting & restoring";
"help.deleting.body" = "Clean My Mac never deletes files permanently. Everything goes to the Trash (on external drives, to that drive’s own Trash). To restore, open the Trash, right-click the file and choose “Put Back”.\n\nSystem folders (/System, /Library, /usr…), your home folder, Documents, Desktop, Downloads, the root of each drive and the app itself are locked.\n\nThe Deletion Log lists everything removed by the app. Space is freed for good only after you empty the Trash.";
"help.faq.title" = "FAQ";
"help.faq.body" = "Why does the total differ from “About This Mac”?\nmacOS counts APFS snapshots, swap and purgeable space that cannot be listed as files. They are shown as “System data & unreadable”.\n\nWhy are some folders locked?\nThey are protected by macOS. Grant Full Disk Access, or they belong to the system and should not be touched.\n\nmacOS says it can’t verify the app (“Apple could not verify…”).\nBuilds from GitHub are signed ad-hoc and not notarized yet. Open System Settings › Privacy & Security, scroll to Security and click “Open Anyway”.\n\nHow do I uninstall?\nDrag Clean My Mac from Applications to the Trash. Settings and the deletion log live in ~/Library/Application Support/CleanMyMac.\n\nWhich macOS versions are supported?\nmacOS 13 Ventura and later, on Apple silicon and Intel Macs.";
